('igot-clean-127', 'Adjudication in GST', '1h 35m', 'Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs(CBIC)', 'The course defines adjudication, its process and the importance of the adjudicating authority. It also outlines the significance of order-in-original and helps understand how it is drafted. It further explains the general guidelines to be followed while adjudicating a case under GST. The course also elucidates the concept of Show Cause Notice (SCN), which is an important part of adjudication.', 'iGoT Karmayogi', 'Adjudication in GST', 'Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs(CBIC)', 'The course defines adjudication, its process and the importance of the adjudicating authority. It also outlines the significance of order-in-original and helps understand how it is drafted. It further explains the general guidelines to be followed while adjudicating a case under GST. The course also elucidates the concept of Show Cause Notice (SCN), which is an important part of adjudication.', true, NOW()),
('igot-clean-128', 'e-Ustad: Negotiation Skills', '1h', 'Bureau of Police Research and Development BPRD', 'Negotiation is a process of communication and interaction between two or more parties who have differing interests, goals, or viewpoints. The primary objective of negotiation is to reach a mutually acceptable agreement or resolution that addresses the concerns and needs of all parties involved. Negotiation is a crucial skill for law enforcement professionals, as it plays a significant role in resolving conflicts, managing crises, and ensuring public safety. Law enforcement officers today face a myriad of challenges that extend beyond the realm of enforcing laws. As societies become more complicated, officers need to change the way they handle things. They have to learn how to handle arguments, calm down tense situations, and work with different groups of people. Sometimes, simply using their power and authority isn''t enough. This is where negotiation skills come into play. By honing these skills, officers can navigate complex interactions with greater finesse, leading to peaceful resolution that prioritises both public safety and individual rights. Law enforcement officers furthermore often encounter high-stress situations that require rapid decision- making. Effective negotiation skills can de-escalate volatile situations by allowing officers to establish rapport, understand the emotions and motivations of those involved, and find non-violent solutions. This can prevent conflicts from spiralling into violent confrontations, minimising harm to all parties.', 'iGoT Karmayogi', 'e-Ustad: Negotiation Skills', 'Bureau of Police Research and Development BPRD', 'Negotiation is a process of communication and interaction between two or more parties who have differing interests, goals, or viewpoints. The primary objective of negotiation is to reach a mutually acceptable agreement or resolution that addresses the concerns and needs of all parties involved. Negotiation is a crucial skill for law enforcement professionals, as it plays a significant role in resolving conflicts, managing crises, and ensuring public safety. Law enforcement officers today face a myriad of challenges that extend beyond the realm of enforcing laws. As societies become more complicated, officers need to change the way they handle things. They have to learn how to handle arguments, calm down tense situations, and work with different groups of people. Sometimes, simply using their power and authority isn''t enough. This is where negotiation skills come into play. By honing these skills, officers can navigate complex interactions with greater finesse, leading to peaceful resolution that prioritises both public safety and individual rights. Law enforcement officers furthermore often encounter high-stress situations that require rapid decision- making. Effective negotiation skills can de-escalate volatile situations by allowing officers to establish rapport, understand the emotions and motivations of those involved, and find non-violent solutions. This can prevent conflicts from spiralling into violent confrontations, minimising harm to all parties.', true, NOW()),
('igot-clean-129', 'e-Ustad: POCSO Act', '2h', 'Bureau of Police Research and Development BPRD', 'Child Sexual Abuse (CSA) is a grave violation of human rights and poses a serious threat to the well-being and safety of children. It is the most heinous crime against children and in most instances, it is hidden in nature and also it is the most under-reported crime. CSA is largely ignored by the public and criminal justice system. In India, CSA was not publicly acknowledged as a criminal offence. It was only after the enactment of new legislation called ''The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012 (POCSO Act)'' that criminalised a range of acts including child rape, harassment and exploitation of pornography. The POCSO Act is a dedicated legal framework designed to address the alarming issue of CSA. India is home to over 19% of the world''s children or 42% (more than one-third) of India''s total population. Approx. 50% of these children are in need of care and protection. The POCSO Act is a landmark legislation that specifically focuses on the protection of children from sexual offences. It defines a child as any person below the age of 18 years and recognises the unique vulnerability of children to sexual abuse and exploitation. It aims to create a safe and secure environment for children by preventing and punishing those who commit sexual offences against them.', 'iGoT Karmayogi', 'e-Ustad: POCSO Act', 'Bureau of Police Research and Development BPRD', 'Child Sexual Abuse (CSA) is a grave violation of human rights and poses a serious threat to the well-being and safety of children. It is the most heinous crime against children and in most instances, it is hidden in nature and also it is the most under-reported crime. CSA is largely ignored by the public and criminal justice system. In India, CSA was not publicly acknowledged as a criminal offence. It was only after the enactment of new legislation called ''The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012 (POCSO Act)'' that criminalised a range of acts including child rape, harassment and exploitation of pornography. The POCSO Act is a dedicated legal framework designed to address the alarming issue of CSA. India is home to over 19% of the world''s children or 42% (more than one-third) of India''s total population. Approx. 50% of these children are in need of care and protection. The POCSO Act is a landmark legislation that specifically focuses on the protection of children from sexual offences. It defines a child as any person below the age of 18 years and recognises the unique vulnerability of children to sexual abuse and exploitation. It aims to create a safe and secure environment for children by preventing and punishing those who commit sexual offences against them.', true, NOW()),
('igot-clean-130', 'e-Ustad: Soft Skills and Communication Skills', '1h', 'Bureau of Police Research and Development BPRD', 'The police department is one of the most important institutions in any society. The police are primarily responsible for maintaining public safety by enforcing laws, protecting lives and property, and providing emergency assistance when needed. However, in recent years, the police have faced increased criticism for their handling of various situations. One of the primary issues is the lack of soft skills. Soft skills refer to interpersonal skills that enable individuals to communicate effectively, work collaboratively, and build relationships with others. These skills are crucial in the context of policing because police officers regularly interact with people from diverse backgrounds and cultures. According to research conducted at Harvard and Stanford Universities, only 15% of your career success is provided by your hard skills, while the other 85% is provided by so-called soft skills. ''Soft Skills'' correlates with some terms of very close meaning: ''Life Skills'', ''Emotional Intelligence Quotients'', ''Social Skills'' and ''Interpersonal Skills''. Further, the soft skills are broken down into categories, or types of skills according to the level of complexity and interaction. A learner needs to go through this e-learning course on Soft Skills and Communication Skills for knowing more.', 'iGoT Karmayogi', 'e-Ustad: Soft Skills and Communication Skills', 'Bureau of Police Research and Development BPRD', 'The police department is one of the most important institutions in any society. The police are primarily responsible for maintaining public safety by enforcing laws, protecting lives and property, and providing emergency assistance when needed. However, in recent years, the police have faced increased criticism for their handling of various situations. One of the primary issues is the lack of soft skills. Soft skills refer to interpersonal skills that enable individuals to communicate effectively, work collaboratively, and build relationships with others. These skills are crucial in the context of policing because police officers regularly interact with people from diverse backgrounds and cultures. According to research conducted at Harvard and Stanford Universities, only 15% of your career success is provided by your hard skills, while the other 85% is provided by so-called soft skills. ''Soft Skills'' correlates with some terms of very close meaning: ''Life Skills'', ''Emotional Intelligence Quotients'', ''Social Skills'' and ''Interpersonal Skills''. Further, the soft skills are broken down into categories, or types of skills according to the level of complexity and interaction. A learner needs to go through this e-learning course on Soft Skills and Communication Skills for knowing more.', true, NOW()),
